\insrsid2967002 {\*\bkmkstart topic_Using_VAQL_Virtual_Analyst_Que}{\*\bkmkend topic_Using_VAQL_Virtual_Analyst_Que}Using VAQL (Virtual Analyst Query Language)
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \ltrpar\ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\wrapdefault\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs24\alang1025 \ltrch\fcs0 \fs24\lang3081\langfe3081\cgrid\langnp3081\langfenp3081 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af1\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \f1\fs20\insrsid2967002 
The Virtual Analyst includes a Controlled Natural Language (CNL) that called FEKL (FactEngine Knowledge Language). FEKL is a derivative of Data Constellation's open-source CQL (Constellation Query Language) (http://www.d
ataconstellation.com) available through their Active Facts project (https://github.com/cjheath/activefacts).
\par 
\par CQL is designed so that analysts can create, edit and publish models in a CNL. FactEngine's FEKL derives from this language, using ALLCAPS for reserved words within the language.
\par 
\par A brief example as introduction to FEKL will frame the nature and intent of the language:
\par 
\par The FEKL statement above defines a Value Type called "FirstName", with a Data Type of 'String Fixed Length', with the maximum length of instances of that Value Type being 100 characters long.
\par 
\par \tab "}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af1\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \f1\fs20\cf19\insrsid2967002 FirstName}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af1\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \f1\fs20\cf2\insrsid2967002  IS WRITTEN AS}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af1\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \f1\fs20\insrsid2967002  }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af1\afs20 
\ltrch\fcs0 \f1\fs20\cf15\insrsid2967002 StringFixedLength}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af1\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \f1\fs20\insrsid2967002 (100)"
\par 
\par 
\par While the benefits of defining a Value Type using natural language may not be immediately apparent, it is the functionality of the Virtual Analyst toolbox which brings about the unseen benefits.
\par 
\par Using Auto-Completion, Boston's VA augments rapid model definition by displaying the list of options available to complete a FEKL sentence. The Auto-Completion list is displayed under the sentence that you are typing. This allows you to quickly and effici
ently complete the sentence. An example of the Auto-Completion functionality is displayed below:
\par 
\par }\pard \ltrpar\qc \li0\ri0\widctlpar\wrapdefault\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\field{\*\fldinst {\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af1\afs16 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f1\fs16\insrsid2967002 INCLUDEPICTURE "VAQLISWRITTENASAutoComplete.png" \\* MERGEFORMAT \\d}}{\fldrslt {\rtlch\fcs1 
\ab\af1\afs16 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f1\fs16\insrsid4737885 }}}\sectd \linex0\sectdefaultcl\sftnbj {\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af1\afs16 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f1\fs16\insrsid2967002 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af1\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f1\fs20\insrsid2967002 Auto-Completion in Boston
\par }\pard \ltrpar\ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\wrapdefault\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af1\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \f1\fs20\insrsid2967002  
\par The result of entering the verifiable FEKL statement (above) is either creation of a new Value Type, or modifying of an existing Value Type within the model.
\par 
\par We estimate that using the VA and FEKL is 2 to 3 times faster than using the standard drag-and-drop features to modify a model within Boston.
\par 
\par Creation of new
 Value Types, or editing the Data Type of an existing Value Type is a simple example. A more complex example, model-wise, but just as easy to type into the VA, is that of creating a new Fact Type with accompanying Entity Type and Value Type all within one
 sentence:
\par 
\par \tab "}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af1\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \f1\fs20\cf19\insrsid2967002 Person}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af1\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \f1\fs20\insrsid2967002  }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af1\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \f1\fs20\cf21\insrsid2967002 has}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af1\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 
\f1\fs20\insrsid2967002  }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af1\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \f1\fs20\cf2\insrsid2967002 ONE}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af1\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \f1\fs20\insrsid2967002  }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af1\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \f1\fs20\cf19\insrsid2967002 FirstName}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af1\afs20 
\ltrch\fcs0 \f1\fs20\insrsid2967002 "
\par 
\par This simple sentence exemplifies the speed and grace by which models can be edited using the VA. Typing "Person has ONE FirstName" into the VA will set the VA in motion to create an Entity Type for 'Person' (if the '
Person' Entity Type doesn't already exist within the model), a Value Type for 'FirstName', a Fact Type with a reading of 'Person has FirstName', a mandatory constraint and a uniqueness constraint...all defined within that simple sentence. The resulting OR
M model fragment that is created within Boston is shown below:
\par 
\par }\pard \ltrpar\qc \li0\ri0\widctlpar\wrapdefault\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\field{\*\fldinst {\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af1\afs16 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f1\fs16\insrsid2967002 INCLUDEPICTURE "ModelPersonHasONEFirstName.png" \\* MERGEFORMAT \\d}}{\fldrslt {\rtlch\fcs1 
