\rtlch\fcs1 \af1\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \f1\fs20\insrsid2967002  or software source code. Database Definition Language can be for any database type.
\par 
\par The key elements of the Code Generator are:
\par 
\par 1. Projects;
\par 2. Templates;
\par 3. Sources; and
\par 4. Transformations;
\par 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af1\afs26 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f1\fs26\insrsid2967002 Projects
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af1\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \f1\fs20\insrsid2967002 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af1\afs22 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f1\fs22\insrsid2967002 File Management
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af1\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \f1\fs20\insrsid2967002 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af1\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f1\fs20\insrsid2967002 Format
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af1\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \f1\fs20\insrsid2967002 
\par A project is one single file in XML format. A project will have multiple file items, but they are serialised into a single XML format.
\par 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af1\afs26 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f1\fs26\insrsid2967002 Sources
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af1\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \f1\fs20\insrsid2967002 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af1\afs22 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f1\fs22\insrsid2967002 What is a source?
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af1\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \f1\fs20\insrsid2967002 
\par A source defines a connection to a database or Boston model and the associated meta data to retrieve from that model and from which to generate text output.
\par 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af1\afs26 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f1\fs26\insrsid2967002 Templates
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af1\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \f1\fs20\insrsid2967002 
\par Templates are used to define how the Code Generator connects to the source and what text to produce based on the schema exposed by a source. Templates output generated text to a file
\par 
\par Providers
\par Model providers are implementations of specific meta data sources (such as different database systems). Each provider would have its own particular quirks, but for database providers there are four components.
\par 
\par Connection
\par 
\par Basically the connection string to the database.
\par 
\par Tables/Views Meta Data
\par 
\par This is meta data for tables and views. There are two ways to retrieve it (under the radio button selection group \'93approach\'94) although some providers may restrict the user to only one of these:
\par 
\par Single result set.
\par 
\par Using a SQL query the meta data is returned as a single data set in order of tables/views and corresponding columns with their information (type, size, precision, etc).
\par Table/column retrieval
\par Tables are retrieved first, and the columns for each table are then retrieved individually. This supports any restrictions by providers that can\rquote t or don\rquote t have column meta data but do have table meta data.
\par Routines Meta Data
\par 
\par This is for stored procedures and functions. SQL queries are the only known way to retrieve them. The result set returned in a single 
data set in order of stored procedures/functions and corresponding parameters with their information(type, size, precision, etc). Any select routines returning a dataset will have the column meta data retrieved internally without user specification. The .
NET framework has support for this.
\par 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af1\afs22 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f1\fs22\insrsid2967002 Managed Code
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af1\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \f1\fs20\insrsid2967002 
\par Templates can execute .Net code. The source is in managed code files supporting either Visual Basic or C Sharp. Code files are brought together and compiled into an individual assembly. One assembly for VB code, and a separate assembly for C# code.
\par 
\par This is possible via .Net codeDOM.
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \ltrpar\s2\ql \li0\ri0\keepn\widctlpar\tx1200\wrapdefault\faauto\outlinelevel1\rin0\lin0\itap0 \rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af1\afs24\alang1025 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f1\fs24\ul\lang1252\langfe3081\cgrid\langnp1252\langfenp3081 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af1 \ltrch\fcs0 
\insrsid2967002 {\*\bkmkstart topic_Code_Generator_Templates}{\*\bkmkend topic_Code_Generator_Templates}Templates
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \ltrpar\ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\wrapdefault\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs24\alang1025 \ltrch\fcs0 \fs24\lang3081\langfe3081\cgrid\langnp3081\langfenp3081 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af1\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \f1\fs20\insrsid2967002 
Enter topic text here.
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \ltrpar\s3\ql \li0\ri0\keepn\widctlpar\tx1200\wrapdefault\faauto\outlinelevel2\rin0\lin0\itap0 \rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af1\afs24\alang1025 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f1\fs24\ul\lang1252\langfe3081\cgrid\langnp1252\langfenp3081 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af1 \ltrch\fcs0 
\insrsid2967002 {\*\bkmkstart topic_Code_Generator_Template_Headers}{\*\bkmkend topic_Code_Generator_Template_Headers}Template Headers
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \ltrpar\ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\wrapdefault\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs24\alang1025 \ltrch\fcs0 \fs24\lang3081\langfe3081\cgrid\langnp3081\langfenp3081 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af1\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \f1\fs20\insrsid2967002 
\par Template headers are defined using a header block containing specifiers for three main template behaviours:
\par 
\par }\pard \ltrpar\ql \fi-225\li225\ri0\widctlpar\tx225\wrapdefault\faauto\rin0\lin225\itap0 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af1\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \f1\fs20\insrsid2967002 1.\tab The template name;}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f1\insrsid2967002 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af1\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \f1\fs20\insrsid2967002 2.\tab What kind of multiple output; and}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f1\insrsid2967002 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af1\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \f1\fs20\insrsid2967002 3.\tab The output file path.}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f1\insrsid2967002 
\par }\pard \ltrpar\ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\wrapdefault\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af1\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \f1\fs20\insrsid2967002  
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af1\afs26 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f1\fs26\insrsid2967002 Definition
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af1\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \f1\fs20\insrsid2967002 
\par header
\par   is [template_name](argument,list)
\par   [for_statement]
\par   return [output_expression]
\par end
\par Description
\par header
\par Start of the header block.
\par template_name \endash  identifier
\par 
\par The template is named here using the is keyword and is the identifier for when it is called. The template item name in the project is not the identifier, and although they are generally the same name, they do not have to be identical.
\par 
\par Parameters are declared within parenthesis. Parenthesis can be omitted if the are no parameters, otherwise empty parenthesis indicate no parameters.
\par for_statement \endash  statement
\par If omitted, the output is a single file according to the return expression. If a for statement is specified, the output is multiple according to the return expression (as a rule the return expression makes use of the for iterator).
\par output_expression \endash  string
\par 
\par The output path. Paths are relative to the location of the Metadrone project file not the executable.
\par 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af1\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f1\fs20\insrsid2967002 end
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af1\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \f1\fs20\insrsid2967002 Terminates the header block.
\par For example, for a single file output writing to the file \'93the-output.txt\'94:
\par 
\par header
\par   is Template1
\par   return "the-output.txt"
\par end
\par 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af1\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f1\fs20\insrsid2967002 Call}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af1\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \f1\fs20\insrsid2967002  
\par 
\par The template can be called by using the }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af1\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f1\fs20\insrsid2967002 call}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af1\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \f1\fs20\insrsid2967002  statement:
\par 
\par E.g. call template1
\par  
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af1\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f1\fs20\insrsid2967002 For multiple outputs:
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af1\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \f1\fs20\insrsid2967002 (note the use of the for iterator variable in the return expression).
\par 
\par E.g.
\par 
\par header
\par   is Template2(connection)
\par   for table tbl in connection
\par   return "the-output-" + tbl + ".txt"
\par end
\par 
\par E.g.
\par 
\par A template is called and a parameter passed to the template:
\par 
\par call Template2(sources.Source1)
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \ltrpar\s3\ql \li0\ri0\keepn\widctlpar\tx1200\wrapdefault\faauto\outlinelevel2\rin0\lin0\itap0 \rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af1\afs24\alang1025 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f1\fs24\ul\lang1252\langfe3081\cgrid\langnp1252\langfenp3081 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af1 \ltrch\fcs0 
\insrsid2967002 {\*\bkmkstart topic_Code_Generator_Template_SafeZones}{\*\bkmkend topic_Code_Generator_Template_SafeZones}Safe Zones
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \ltrpar\ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\wrapdefault\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs24\alang1025 \ltrch\fcs0 \fs24\lang3081\langfe3081\cgrid\langnp3081\langfenp3081 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af1\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \f1\fs20\insrsid2967002 
To avoid having customisations overwritten by regeneration, content can be preserved within safe zones.
\par 
\par For example here is some sample generated code:
\par 
\par     public double Seconds
\par \tab \{
\par \tab \tab get \{ return _Seconds; \}
\par \tab \tab set \{ _Seconds = value; \}
\par \tab \}
\par \tab public string Location
\par \tab \{
\par \tab \tab get \{ return _Location; \}
\par \tab \tab set \{ _Location = value; \}
\par \tab \}
\par Defining safe zone identifiers in main:
\par 
\par #safebegin = "//safe start"
\par #safeend = "//safe end"
\par 
\par And then in the generated output file, add customisations within safe zones:
\par 
\par     public double Seconds
\par \tab \{
\par \tab \tab get \{ return _Seconds; \}
\par \tab \tab set \{ _Seconds = value; \}
\par \tab \}
\par //safe start
\par \tab public double Hours
\par \tab \{
\par \tab \tab get \{ return Seconds / 3600; \}
\par \tab \tab set \{ Seconds = value * 3600; \}
\par \tab \}
\par \tab public double Minutes
\par \tab \{
\par \tab \tab get \{ return Seconds / 60; \}
\par \tab \tab set \{ Seconds = value * 60; \}
\par \tab \}
\par \tab public double Minutes
\par \tab \{
\par \tab \tab get \{ return Seconds; \}
\par \tab \tab set \{ Seconds = value; \}
\par \tab \}
\par //safe end
\par \tab public string Location
\par \tab \{
\par \tab \tab get \{ return _Location; \}
\par \tab \tab set \{ _Location = value; \}
\par \tab \}
\par  
\par 
\par 
\par 
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \ltrpar\s3\ql \li0\ri0\keepn\widctlpar\tx1200\wrapdefault\faauto\outlinelevel2\rin0\lin0\itap0 \rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af1\afs24\alang1025 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f1\fs24\ul\lang1252\langfe3081\cgrid\langnp1252\langfenp3081 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af1 \ltrch\fcs0 
\insrsid2967002 {\*\bkmkstart topic_Code_Generator_Template_Syntax}{\*\bkmkend topic_Code_Generator_Template_Syntax}Template Syntax
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \ltrpar\ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\wrapdefault\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs24\alang1025 \ltrch\fcs0 \fs24\lang3081\langfe3081\cgrid\langnp3081\langfenp3081 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af1\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \f1\fs20\insrsid2967002 
Objective of Templates
\par 
\par The aim of each Template is to define how text is output by the Code Generator. This section outlines the syntax of the language used within Templates to define how text is output.\line 
For more sophisticated programming needs, managed .Net code can be executed within the script (see runcs or runvb).
\par 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af1\afs26 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f1\fs26\insrsid2967002 The key principals. Template Code vs Plain Text
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af1\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \f1\fs20\insrsid2967002 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af1\afs22 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f1\fs22\insrsid2967002 Tags
\par 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af1\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \f1\fs20\insrsid2967002 Tags are used to enclose text to output, variable data to output or further tags. In essence, tags control the logic of the Template.
\par The plain text outside of the <<! and !>> tags are output as the text is. This included line-feeds and the comma. Plain text is to be enclosed in double quotes (").
\par 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af1\afs22 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f1\fs22\insrsid2967002 Comments
\par 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af1\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \f1\fs20\insrsid2967002 Any text preceded with a double forward slash \'93//\'94 on a line are ignored by the compiler. There is no support for multi-line commenting (such as a /* a comment like this */).
\par 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af1\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f1\fs20\insrsid2967002 The For loop
\par 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af1\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \f1\fs20\insrsid2967002 For loops are used to iterate through the list of items associated with a schema component.
\par E.g. This sample code looping through tables and columns within each table and example output:
\par 
\par <<!for table tbl in sources.source1!>>
\par SELECT
\par   <<!for column col in tbl!>>
\par   <<!col!>>,
\par   <<!end!>>
\par FROM <<!tbl!>>
\par <<!end!>>
\par 
\par ...can return this output:
\par 
\par SELECT
\par   FirstName,
\par   LastName,
\par   Address1,
\par   Address2,
\par FROM Customer
\par 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af1\afs22 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f1\fs22\insrsid2967002 Statement Separation
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af1\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \f1\fs20\insrsid2967002 
\par Statements are separated by a newline. To have more than one statement on a line, each statement can be separated by a semicolon \'93;\'94.
\par 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af1\afs22 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f1\fs22\insrsid2967002 Variables and Typing
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af1\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \f1\fs20\insrsid2967002 Variables are declared by using the set keyword. There is no explicit typing, 
variable types are determined dynamically. Additionally there are no classes, enumerations, etc. The system types provided are:
\par 
\par Numeric
\par String
\par Boolean
\par Source
\par Model connection used by the for keyword.
\par Meta
\par Model components (such as table or column) used by the for keyword.
\par  
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af1\afs22 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f1\fs22\insrsid2967002 Function Calls
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af1\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \f1\fs20\insrsid2967002 
\par There are no function calls, however templates can be treated as functions and are run by using the }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af1\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f1\fs20\insrsid2967002 call }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af1\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \f1\fs20\insrsid2967002 command.
\par 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af1\afs22 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f1\fs22\insrsid2967002 Scope of variables
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af1\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \f1\fs20\insrsid2967002 
\par There is a simple variable scope concept, without any object oriented information hiding principles deliberately being used. There are no access modifiers such as public, private, friend, etc. Three different kinds of references have this behaviour.

\par 
\par }\pard \ltrpar\ql \fi-225\li225\ri0\widctlpar\tx225\wrapdefault\faauto\rin0\lin225\itap0 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af1\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \f1\fs20\insrsid2967002 1.\tab Sources\line Sources have global
 exposure and can be called anywhere. A source connection is referenced by qualifying with the reserved word sources.\line }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f1\insrsid2967002 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af1\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \f1\fs20\insrsid2967002 2.\tab Templates\line Calls to templates are only accessible within its own package.\line }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f1\insrsid2967002 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af1\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \f1\fs20\insrsid2967002 3.\tab Variables\line Variables are visible only within the block con
struct it has been declared in. For instance a variable declared within a for loop (including the variable declared as the iterator), is not accessible outside of the block (after the end statement).}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f1\insrsid2967002 

\par }\pard \ltrpar\ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\wrapdefault\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af1\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \f1\fs20\insrsid2967002 
\par Preprocessor Directives
\par In Main, text preceded by a hash \'93#\'94 symbol indicates a directive. Directives apply to the scope of the package. This includes processing of transformations from within that package. Directives cannot be specified in templates.
\par 
\par 
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \ltrpar\s4\ql \li0\ri0\keepn\widctlpar\tx1200\wrapdefault\faauto\outlinelevel3\rin0\lin0\itap0 \rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af1\afs24\alang1025 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f1\fs24\ul\lang1252\langfe3081\cgrid\langnp1252\langfenp3081 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af1 \ltrch\fcs0 
\insrsid2967002 {\*\bkmkstart topic_Code_Generator_Preprocessor_Direct}{\*\bkmkend topic_Code_Generator_Preprocessor_Direct}Preprocessor Directives
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \ltrpar\ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\wrapdefault\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs24\alang1025 \ltrch\fcs0 \fs24\lang3081\langfe3081\cgrid\langnp3081\langfenp3081 {\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af1\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f1\fs20\insrsid2967002 #ignorecase

\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af1\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \f1\fs20\insrsid2967002 
\par Specifies case sensitivity for string comparisons. The switch is either \'93on\'94 or \'93off. If not specified the default is \'93on\'94.
\par 
\par E.g.
\par 
\par #ignorecase on //String comparisons are not case sensitive.
\par #ignorecase off //String comparisons are case sensitive.
\par  
\par 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af1\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f1\fs20\insrsid2967002 #safebegin and #safeend
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af1\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \f1\fs20\insrsid2967002 
\par Specify safe zone identifiers.
\par 
\par E.g.
\par 
\par #safebegin = "// safe begin"
\par #safeend = "// safe end"
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \ltrpar\s4\ql \li0\ri0\keepn\widctlpar\tx1200\wrapdefault\faauto\outlinelevel3\rin0\lin0\itap0 \rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af1\afs24\alang1025 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f1\fs24\ul\lang1252\langfe3081\cgrid\langnp1252\langfenp3081 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af1 \ltrch\fcs0 
\insrsid2967002 {\*\bkmkstart topic_Code_Generator_Statement_Call}{\*\bkmkend topic_Code_Generator_Statement_Call}Call
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \ltrpar\ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\wrapdefault\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs24\alang1025 \ltrch\fcs0 \fs24\lang3081\langfe3081\cgrid\langnp3081\langfenp3081 {\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af1\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f1\fs20\insrsid2967002 Call
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af1\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \f1\fs20\insrsid2967002 
\par Execute a template by calling it. There is no return value when calling a template.
\par 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af1\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f1\fs20\insrsid2967002 Definition
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af1\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \f1\fs20\insrsid2967002 
\par call [template_name](argument,list)
\par 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af1\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f1\fs20\insrsid2967002 Description
\par 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af1\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \f1\fs20\insrsid2967002 template_name \endash  identifier. The template to execute as identified in its header.
\par argument list - Arguments are passed within parenthesis. Parenthesis can be omitted if the are no arguments, otherwise empty parenthesis indicate no arguments.
\par  
\par E.g.
\par 
\par call template1
\par call template2(tablevar,colvar)
\par 
\par 
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \ltrpar\s4\ql \li0\ri0\keepn\widctlpar\tx1200\wrapdefault\faauto\outlinelevel3\rin0\lin0\itap0 \rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af1\afs24\alang1025 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f1\fs24\ul\lang1252\langfe3081\cgrid\langnp1252\langfenp3081 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af1 \ltrch\fcs0 
\insrsid2967002 {\*\bkmkstart topic_Code_Generator_Statement_Clcon}{\*\bkmkend topic_Code_Generator_Statement_Clcon}Clcon
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \ltrpar\ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\wrapdefault\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs24\alang1025 \ltrch\fcs0 \fs24\lang3081\langfe3081\cgrid\langnp3081\langfenp3081 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af1\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \f1\fs20\insrsid2967002 
Clears the debug console.
\par 
\par Definition
\par clcon()
\par Parameters:
\par No arguments.
\par Returns:
\par Null.
\par  
\par 
\par E.g.
\par 
\par <<!clcon!>>
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \ltrpar\s4\ql \li0\ri0\keepn\widctlpar\tx1200\wrapdefault\faauto\outlinelevel3\rin0\lin0\itap0 \rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af1\afs24\alang1025 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f1\fs24\ul\lang1252\langfe3081\cgrid\langnp1252\langfenp3081 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af1 \ltrch\fcs0 
\insrsid2967002 {\*\bkmkstart topic_Code_Generator_Statement_Cout_and_}{\*\bkmkend topic_Code_Generator_Statement_Cout_and_}Cout and Coutln
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \ltrpar\ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\wrapdefault\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs24\alang1025 \ltrch\fcs0 \fs24\lang3081\langfe3081\cgrid\langnp3081\langfenp3081 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af1\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \f1\fs20\insrsid2967002 
Output a string to the debug console. Cout will output a string expression. Coutln will output a string expression followed by a new line.
\par 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af1\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f1\fs20\insrsid2967002 Definition
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af1\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \f1\fs20\insrsid2967002 
\par cout(arg1)
\par 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af1\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f1\fs20\insrsid2967002 Parameters
\par 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af1\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \f1\fs20\insrsid2967002 arg1 \endash  string. The string expression to use.
\par 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af1\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f1\fs20\insrsid2967002 Returns
\par 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af1\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \f1\fs20\insrsid2967002 arg1
\par  
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af1\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f1\fs20\insrsid2967002 Definition
\par 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af1\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \f1\fs20\insrsid2967002 coutln(arg1)
\par 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af1\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f1\fs20\insrsid2967002 Parameters
\par 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af1\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \f1\fs20\insrsid2967002 arg1 \endash  string. The string expression to use.
\par 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af1\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f1\fs20\insrsid2967002 Returns
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af1\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \f1\fs20\insrsid2967002 arg1
\par 
\par E.g.
\par 
\par <<!
\par coutln("current table is" + tbl)
\par !>>
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \ltrpar\s4\ql \li0\ri0\keepn\widctlpar\tx1200\wrapdefault\faauto\outlinelevel3\rin0\lin0\itap0 \rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af1\afs24\alang1025 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f1\fs24\ul\lang1252\langfe3081\cgrid\langnp1252\langfenp3081 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af1 \ltrch\fcs0 
\insrsid2967002 {\*\bkmkstart topic_Code_Generator_Statement_Command}{\*\bkmkend topic_Code_Generator_Statement_Command}Command
